<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1840–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rwabugiri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri was mwami (king) of Rwanda in the second half of the 19th century and is regarded as its most powerful pre-colonial ruler. He centralised royal authority, weakened lineage chiefs, extended the system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> clientage and led many military campaigns against neighbouring lands. He died in 1895, shortly before Rwanda came under German rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri.jpg" alt="Kigeli IV Rwabugiri" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mwami_Kigeri_IV_Rwabugiri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rwabugiri, whose birth name is given as Sezisoni Rwabugiri, was a Tutsi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya dynasty</a>, which traced its line back to Gihanga, one of the first kings whose deeds are celebrated in Rwanda's oral chronicles. His year of birth is uncertain and is given as about 1840. He held authority from 1867 to 1895 and was among the last kings of his line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around 1870 Rwabugiri established a royal court at Rubengera that collected labour dues and tribute in food. It channelled food across the country and became a centre of commerce. The court managed production so as to keep a surplus as a provision against famine, and on the king's order this was distributed to the poorest in exchange for their labour; in times of shortage, however, the very rich Tutsi living at Rubengera could find food and livestock while most of the country suffered.</p><p>As he annexed new lands he placed appointed chiefs over them, drawn into a network that bound the regions to the court. These chiefs sometimes met local resistance: in the north-west the Balera challenged the Nduga whom the court had installed, a contest along lines of clan, since both groups were then counted as Tutsi. Several annexed districts, such as Bugoyi, Bwishaza and Kingogo, had been inhabited entirely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>, and Tutsi moved into them in numbers afterwards. He also killed chiefs of his own Nyiginya lineage who had shown too much independence.</p><p>He was the first king of Rwanda to come into contact with Europeans. He built an army equipped with guns obtained from Germans and barred most foreigners, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, from the kingdom, defending its borders against neighbouring kingdoms and slave traders. By the end of his rule the country was divided into provinces, districts, hills and neighbourhoods under a hierarchy of chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rwanda had been assigned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1890, but no expedition came until that of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen">Gustav Adolf von Götzen</a> in 1894. Rwabugiri died in September 1895 on an expedition in what is now Congo. His son Rutarindwa was proclaimed king as Mibambwe IV, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanjogera">Kanjogera</a> of the Bega clan, another of Rwabugiri's wives, as queen mother. With her brother Kabare she carried out a coup at Rucunshu in which Rutarindwa was killed, and made her own son king as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a>. The weakened state opened the way to direct German colonisation in 1897.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kigeli IV Rwabugiri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rwanda under <b>Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</b> was a dangerous enemy, and Rwandan armies raided Nkore in the late nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The greatest warrior king was <b>Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</b>, who fought almost every year, invaded Ijwi Island in Lake Kivu, raided Bushi to the west and Nkore to the north-east, and died in 1895 while on campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ruled by the Tutsi Nyiginya clan, it dominated from the mid-18th century and reached its greatest extent under Kigeli <b>Rwabugiri</b> in the 19th, whose ubuhake clientage and uburetwa corvée opened a rift between Hutu and Tutsi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Rwanda. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
